  "meta": {
    "title": "Some letter appears twice",
    "blurb": "Over {a, b, c}: accepts when any letter occurs at least twice, anywhere. The NFA guesses the letter and waits for its twin — five states. A DFA has to remember which letters it has seen, and needs nine.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "aa",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "abca",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "bcb",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "abcc",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "abc",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "cab",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "ba",
        "expect": "reject"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "guessing",
        "nfa-vs-dfa",
        "subset-construction"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "readme": "A deterministic machine for this language has to carry the *set* of letters seen so far: ∅, {a}, {b}, {c}, {a,b}, {a,c}, {b,c}, {a,b,c} — eight sets — plus an accepting state for \"a repeat has happened\". The nondeterministic one carries a single letter, the one it has bet on.\n\nWith *k* letters the gap is *k* + 2 states against 2ᵏ + 1. Note that every word of length four or more is accepted (pigeonhole), so the language is finite in its complement: only 16 words are rejected."
    }
  }
}
